It is easy to see that if there exists a $[2,2]$-GDC$(6)$ of
type $2^{3t+1}$ with size $2t(3t+1)$, then there is an optimal
$(6t+2,6,[2,2])_3$-code. So we will construct
$[2,2]$-GDC$(6)$s of type $2^{3t+1}$.



\begin{lemma}
\label{s2^{3t+1}} There exists a $[2,2]$-GDC$(6)$ of type
$2^{3t+1}$ with size $2t(3t+1)$ for each $3\leq t\leq 11$ or
$t\in\{14,17\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} For $t=3$, the code is constructed in Lemma~\ref{[2,2]-GDC:2^10}.

For $4\leq t\leq 11$ or
$t\in\{14,17\}$, let $X_t=\bbZ_{6t+2}$, and ${\cal
G}_t=\{\{i,i+3t+1\}:0\leq i\leq 3t\}$. Then $(X_t,{\cal
G}_t,{\cal C}_t)$ is the desired $[2,2]$-GDC$(6)$ of type $2^{3t+1}$, where for $t\neq 5$, ${\cal C}_t$ is obtained
by developing the elements of $\bbZ_{6t+2}$ in the codewords
listed in Table~\ref{t2^{3t+1}} $+1\pmod{6t+2}$, and ${\cal
C}_5$ is obtained by developing the elements of $\bbZ_{32}$ in
the codewords listed in Table~\ref{t2^{3t+1}} $+2\pmod{32}$.
\end{proof}

